The equilibrium constant $K_{c}$ is the ratio of the concentrations of products and reactants. As concentration is related to pressure through the ideal gas law, we can also express an equilibrium constant in terms of pressures; we call this $K_{p}$. Let us consider, again, a general equilibrium reaction in which $A_1$ and $A_2$ react to form $A_3$ and $A_4$
\begin{center}\ce{$\underbrace{\textcolor{red}{\ce{$\nu_1$A_1_{(g)} + $\nu_2$A_2_{(g)}}}}_{\textrm{Reactants}}$ <=> $\underbrace{\textcolor{blue}{\ce{$\nu_3$A_3_{(g)} + $\nu_4$A_4_{(g)}}}}_{\textrm{Products}}$}
\end{center}
The stoichiometric coefficients of the reaction are $\nu_1$, $\nu_2$, $\nu_3$, and $\nu_4$. Hence, the expression of the equilibrium constant in terms of pressures will be:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{  K_{p}=\frac{P_{ \text{Products} } }{P_{  \text{Reactants} }  }=\frac{  P_{A_3}^{\nu_3} \cdot P_{A_4}^{\nu_4}} {P_{A_1}^{\nu_1} \cdot  P_{A_2}^{\nu_2}   } } \quad \textcolor{blue}{\text{Equilibrium constant in terms of pressures}}\label{\chapterlabel:equation2}
\end{equation}
 Let us break down the expression of $K_p$. On top of the fraction, we have the equilibrium pressures of the products to the power of their coefficients. For example, $P_{A_3}^{\nu_3}$ represents the equilibrium pressure of $A_3$ to the power of the coefficient $\nu_3$. In the bottom of the fraction, we have the pressures of the reactants to the power of their coefficients. All concentrations in $K_{p}$ are timed.
